[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small application or file icon with a multicolor circular graphic on a light background. It does not contain scientific panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 2, "caption": "Fig. 1 | Chroma is a generative model for proteins and protein complexes that combines structured diffusion for protein backbones with scalable molecular neural networks for backbone synthesis and all-atom design. a , A correlated diffusion process with chain and radius-of-gyration constraints gradually transforms protein structures into random collapsed polymers (right to left). The reverse process (left to right) can be expressed in terms of a time-dependent optimal denoiser ̂ t ( , ) θ t x x that maps noisy coordinates x t at time t to predicted denoised coordinates 0 x . b , We parameterize this in terms", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific schematic labeled **a**, **b**, and **c** describing a protein/polymer diffusion-based design workflow.\n\nPanel **a** shows a progression from a **collapsed polymer system** through multiple increasingly organized colored 3D polymer/protein-like structures. Arrows indicate **Training: forwards polymer diffusion** from structured complex toward collapsed polymer, and **Generation: reverse polymer diffusion** toward a **protein complex backbone**. A final arrow labeled **Design network** leads to an **all-atom complex**, with a zoomed inset of atomic protein structure. Visible state labels include **x₁**, **xₜ**, and **x₀**.\n\nPanel **b** shows a **noisy structure xₜ** processed through a **random graph neural network** using **O(N)** or **O(N log[N]) edges**, producing **confidence-weighted predicted inter-residue geometries**. These are passed to an **equivariant geometry solver**, yielding a **predicted denoised structure x̂θ(xₜ, t)**. The biological objects are multichain protein-like complexes rendered as colored chains or residue clouds.\n\nPanel **c** presents a probabilistic model interface: **time-dependent prior log[pₜ(x)]** plus **time-dependent conditioner(s) log[pθ(y|x)]** leading by downward arrow to **time-dependent posterior log[pθ(x|y)]**. Conditioner icons and labels shown are **Symmetry**, **Substructure**, **Shape**, and **Semantics**.\n\nOverall, the figure depicts a generative protein-complex backbone/all-atom design pipeline using diffusion, graph neural networks, predicted inter-residue geometries, and conditioning objectives."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Fig. 2 | Analysis of unconditional samples reveals diverse geometries that exhibit new higher-order structures and refold in silico. a , A representative set of Chroma-sampled proteins and protein complexes exhibits complex and diverse topologies with high secondary-structure content, including familiar TIM (triose-phosphate isomerase) barrel-like folds (top left), antibodyantigen-like complexes (centre right) and new arrangements of helical bundles and β-sheets. b , c , Despite these qualitative similarities, samples frequently", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about protein structure generation/comparison.\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- **Panel a:** A grid of many predicted or designed protein structures shown as ribbon/cartoon models. Structures include alpha helices, beta sheets, loops, and mixed alpha/beta folds, colored in rainbow gradients along the chain. These appear to be individual protein backbones or domains arranged on a white background.\n\n- **Panel b:** Four example protein structure overlays or nearest-neighbor comparisons. Each subpanel shows a colored ribbon structure superimposed with a pale gray reference structure. Labels report:\n  - “Nearest-neighbour TM: 0.46”\n  - “Nearest-neighbour TM: 0.53”\n  - “Nearest-neighbour TM: 0.50”\n  - “Nearest-neighbour TM: 0.51”\n\n- **Panel c:** Bar chart showing probability or fraction above TM-score cutoffs by protein length bin. Y-axis: `P(TM > cutoff)`. X-axis: `Length (residues)`, with bins roughly `[50-150]`, `[150-250]`, `[250-350]`, `[350-450]`, `[450-550]`, `[550-650]`, `[650-750]`, `[750-850]`, `[850-1000]`. Legend includes TM thresholds:\n  - blue: `TM > 0.5`\n  - orange: `TM > 0.7`\n  - green: `TM > 0.9`\n  The highest probabilities are for shorter proteins, especially for `TM > 0.5`.\n\n- **Panel d:** Four example refolding structure comparisons, again shown as colored ribbon models over pale gray structures. Labels report:\n  - “Refolding TM: 0.84”\n  - “Refolding TM: 0.93”\n  - “Refolding TM: 0.96”\n  - “Refolding TM: 0.91”\n\n- **Panel e:** Scatter plot of `AlphaFold TM score` versus `Length (residues)`. X-axis spans approximately 0 to 1,000 residues. Y-axis spans 0 to 1.0. Points are densely distributed, with many values between about 0.2 and 0.9 and a visible decrease or broadening of high scores for longer sequences.\n\nBiological source objects:\n\n- Protein structures/backbones represented as ribbon diagrams.\n- Secondary structure elements include helices, beta strands/sheets, and connecting loops.\n- The figure compares generated or predicted protein folds against nearest-neighbor/reference/refolded structures using TM-score metrics.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces visible:\n\n- Structural overlay/comparison against nearest neighbors or refolding outputs.\n- Quantitative scoring using TM score and AlphaFold TM score.\n- Length-stratified analysis of structural similarity thresholds.\n\nVisible findings:\n\n- Many generated protein-like structures span diverse fold topologies.\n- Nearest-neighbor TM scores shown in panel b are moderate, around 0.46-0.53.\n- Refolding TM scores in panel d are high, around 0.84-0.96.\n- Shorter proteins have higher probability of exceeding TM thresholds, especially `TM > 0.5`.\n- AlphaFold TM scores vary widely with protein length, with a dense cluster at lower-to-mid scores and fewer very high scores for longer proteins."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 5, "caption": "Fig. 3 | Symmetry, substructure and shape conditioning enable geometric molecular programming. a , Sampling oligomeric structures with arbitrary chain symmetries is possible by using a conditioner that tessellates an asymmetric subunit in the energy function. Cyclic (C n ), dihedral (D n ), tetrahedral (T), octahedral (O) and icosahedral (I) symmetry groups can produce a wide variety of possible homomeric complexes. The right-most protein complex contains 60 subunits and 60,000 total residues, which is enabled by leveraging symmetries and using our subquadratically scaling architecture. b , Conditioning on partial substructure (monochrome) enables", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing computationally generated or visualized protein structures.\n\nPanel **a**: Displays many colorful ribbon/cartoon protein assemblies grouped by symmetry labels, including **C3, C4, C6, C8, D3, D4, D6, D8, T, O, and I**. Each example is rendered as multicolored protein chains arranged into cyclic, dihedral, tetrahedral, octahedral, or icosahedral-like symmetric complexes. The right side includes large spherical or cage-like multimeric assemblies.\n\nPanel **b**: Shows examples of protein fold or motif scaffolds labeled **Human DHFR**, **VHH antibody**, **abb motif**, **Chymotrypsin triad**, and **EF hand**. Each row contains several protein backbone/cartoon renderings with secondary-structure elements such as helices and beta sheets. The panel appears to compare generated or designed structures containing recognizable biological motifs or functional folds.\n\nPanel **c**: Shows many small multicolored protein ribbon assemblies arranged to form alphabet letters and digits, spelling/depicting characters such as **A–Z** and numbers. These appear to be designed protein structures constrained into target shapes.\n\nVisible biological source objects: protein backbones, protein domains, antibody-like VHH fold, human DHFR-like fold, chymotrypsin catalytic-triad motif context, EF-hand calcium-binding motif context.\n\nVisible transformations/model interfaces: no explicit model architecture, equations, plots, or interface diagrams are shown. The figure visually presents protein structure generation/design outputs under symmetry, motif/fold conditioning, and target-shape constraints.\n\nMain visible finding: the figure demonstrates diverse generated protein structures, including symmetric oligomeric assemblies, motif- or fold-containing proteins, and proteins arranged into prescribed letter/number shapes."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 4 | Protein structure classifiers and caption models can bias the sampling process towards user-specified properties. a , Neural networks trained to predict protein properties can bias unconditional samples (top) towards states that optimize predicted properties, such as secondary-structure composition (bottom) indicated by CATH class level codes (C1, Mainly Alpha; C2, Mainly Beta; C3, Alpha Beta). b , A neural network trained to predict CATH topology annotations can routinely drive generation towards samples with high predicted probabilities of the intended class label, which sometimes aligns with our intended fold topology for highly abundant labels. Left, highly abundant Rossmann fold (CATH topology 3.40.50, 14.0% of training set); middle,", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing generated or example protein structures as ribbon/cartoon models.\n\nPanel **a**: Shows protein backbone/ribbon structures under headings **“Unconditioned samples”** and **“Condition on secondary structure.”** The conditioned examples are grouped by secondary-structure class labels **α C1**, **β C2**, and **α + β C3**. Structures are colored in rainbow gradients along the chain and include helices, beta sheets, loops, and mixed folds.\n\nPanel **b**: Shows protein structure samples with conditioning on topology. Top row: **“Unconditioned samples”** with example structures labeled by probabilities such as **P(Rossmann) = 0.06**, **P(Ig) = 0.00**, and **P(β-barrel) = 0.00**. Middle row: **“Condition on CATH topology”** with structures labeled **P(Rossmann) = 0.55**, **P(Ig) = 0.86**, **P(β-barrel) = 0.81**. Bottom row: **“Canonical examples”** with higher/matching probabilities, including **P(Rossmann) = 0.98**, **P(Ig) = 0.59**, and **P(β-barrel) = 0.996**.\n\nPanel **c**: Shows protein structures conditioned by text. Top row: **“Unconditioned samples”** with structures labeled by **CP** scores, including **CP = 4.01** and **CP = 2.21**. Middle row: **“Condition on text caption”** with structures labeled **CP = 4.00** and **CP = 1.73**. Bottom row: **“Canonical examples”** with corresponding examples labeled **CP = 4.17** and **CP = 1.44**.\n\nVisible biological objects are protein tertiary structures represented as colored ribbon diagrams. The figure compares unconditioned generation, conditioning on secondary structure, conditioning on CATH topology, conditioning on text captions, and canonical/reference examples. The apparent finding is that conditioned samples better match specified structural categories or caption-related targets, as indicated by displayed probability or CP scores."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Fig. 5 | Experimental validation of Chroma-designed proteins. a , Protocol for protein design and experimental validation. Unconditional designs: 268 proteins. Semantic conditioning: 12 α-conditioned, 13 β-conditioned, 11 α/β mixtures and 6 with β-barrel topology. See text for details. b , Rank-ordered unconditional Chroma protein solubility scores by the split-GFP assay for 172 tested proteins. Red dots and error bars denote means and standard deviations, respectively, from three biological replicates. c , d , X-ray crystal structures (rainbow) of UNC_079 ( c , 1.1 Å resolution, PDB 8TNM, root-meansquare deviation (RMSD) = 1.1 Å) and UNC_239 ( d , 2.4 Å resolution, PDB 8TNO, RMSD = 1.0 Å) overlaid with Chroma-generated models (grey). Insets", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about protein design and experimental validation.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n\n- **Panel a**: Workflow schematic for protein design and validation. It shows “Sample backbone,” “Design sequence,” and “Select high likelihood (no refolding filter),” followed by experimental validation steps including “Solubility screen (E. coli),” “Protein expression and purification,” “X-ray crystallography,” “Circular dichroism,” and “Differential scanning calorimetry.” It also shows “Unconditional designs 268 proteins” and “Semantic conditioning” categories: “12 α-conditioned,” “13 β-conditioned,” “11 α/β mixture,” and “6 β-barrel topology.”\n- **Panel b**: Rank-ordered solubility plot labeled “Split-GFP bin score (solubility)” versus “Rank-ordered proteins.” It marks “Positive control” near the high-solubility end and “Negative control” near the low-solubility end.\n- **Panel c**: Ribbon/cartoon X-ray crystal structure labeled **UNC_079**, with a small comparison inset labeled “Closest PDB TM = 0.7.”\n- **Panel d**: Ribbon/cartoon X-ray crystal structure labeled **UNC_239**, with a small comparison inset labeled “Closest PDB TM = 0.5.”\n- **Panel e**: Multiple designed protein cartoons labeled **UNC_018, UNC_079, UNC_096, UNC_159, UNC_163, UNC_194, UNC_239**, each with melting temperature values, CD spectra, and secondary-structure bar charts labeled α, β, and Other.\n- **Panel f**: Similar validation display for designs labeled **SEM_018, SEM_30, SEM_91**, with melting temperatures, CD spectra, and secondary-structure content bar charts.\n- **Panel g**: Scatter plot comparing predicted α-helix content from CD against predicted α-helix content from Chroma. Categories include Unconditional, α-conditioned, β-barrel topol., and α/β mixture. Reported correlation: **R² = 0.84**.\n- **Panel h**: Scatter plot comparing predicted β-sheet content from CD against predicted β-sheet content from Chroma. Same category legend. Reported correlation: **R² = 0.51**.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n\n- Designed proteins represented as colored ribbon structures.\n- Protein secondary-structure classes: α-helix, β-sheet, and other.\n- Experimental measurements include E. coli solubility screening, circular dichroism spectra, differential scanning calorimetry melting temperatures, and X-ray crystallography.\n\nModel/interface references:\n\n- The figure refers to protein designs generated under “unconditional” and “semantic conditioning” modes.\n- The scatter plots compare CD-derived secondary-structure estimates to predictions from **Chroma**.\n\nFindings shown visually:\n\n- Many designed proteins are experimentally soluble and structurally characterized.\n- Several designs have measurable thermal stability, with melting temperatures shown around the mid-50s to high-70s °C.\n- Designed secondary-structure content from Chroma correlates with CD-derived estimates, more strongly for α-helical content than β-sheet content."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 9, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image only shows the number “1078” as printed text. It does not contain a scientific figure, panels, labels, biological objects, transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 9, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license badge/icon, not a scientific figure. It contains the “CC” symbol and attribution/person icon with “BY” text."]}]

